<header class="soc-head"><p class="featured-region">Social ladder · 1368–1644 (the late Ming, 16th century)</p><h1 class="soc-title">Social Hierarchy of Ming China</h1><p class="soc-sub">From Emperor to the Enslaved</p></header>
<div class="soc-intro"><figure class="soc-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/imperial-examination"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imperial-examination.jpg" alt="Imperial examination"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/imperial-examination">Imperial examination</a><span class="soc-credit">Qiu Ying (仇英) (attributed) · Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p class="soc-lede">Ming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> ranked its people by an old scheme and by the state’s registers. In Confucian theory there were four occupations, in descending order scholars, farmers, artisans and merchants, and beneath them the “mean people” (jianmin), entertainers and prostitutes among them. Soldiers, monks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a>, servants and slaves fell outside the four. The founder, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Hongwu Emperor</a>, tried to fix everyone in place, in self-sufficient village communities and hereditary soldier households. By the 16th century the scheme had blurred: artisans worked on farms at harvest and farmers sought work in the towns in hard times, the richest merchants were merging with the landholding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> ran through the economy. Office went to men who passed the examinations, and the emperor ruled through them and through tens of thousands of palace eunuchs, over perhaps 160 to 200 million people.</p><p class="soc-facts"><span><b>Period</b> 1368–1644 (the late Ming, 16th century)</span><span><b>People</b> 160–200 million</span><span><b>Ranks</b> 12</span><span><b>Profile</b> <a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a></span></p></div>
<section class="soc-ladder-sec" aria-labelledby="soc-ladder-t"><div class="soc-ladder-head"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-ladder-t">The ladder</h2><div class="soc-scale" role="group" aria-label="How to draw the ladder"><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="0" aria-pressed="true">By rank</button><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="1" aria-pressed="false">To scale</button></div></div><ol class="soc-pyr" data-scale="0"><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:24%;--k:0"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-ruler" href="#rank-1" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Huangdi</span> <span class="soc-en">(The emperor)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">One ruler</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:35%;--k:1"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-est" href="#rank-2" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Wang</span> <span class="soc-en">(Imperial princes)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">Fewer than 1 in 1,000 (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:46%;--k:2"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-est" href="#rank-3" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Shidafu</span> <span class="soc-en">(Officials)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 0.05–0.1% (est.)</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-specialists" href="#rank-4" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Huanguan</span> <span class="soc-en">(Palace eunuchs)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">Fewer than 1 in 1,000</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:57%;--k:3"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-est" href="#rank-5" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Shenshi</span> <span class="soc-en">(The <span class="soc-nw">degree-holding</span> gentry)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 0.2–0.5% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:67%;--k:4"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-specialists soc-est" href="#rank-6" style="--sw:2%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Soldiers and military households</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 1–3% (est.)</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-religious soc-est" href="#rank-7" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Buddhist and Daoist clergy</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 0.2–0.5% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:78%;--k:5"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-est" href="#rank-8" style="--sw:86%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Nong</span> <span class="soc-en">(Farmers)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">About 86% (the rest, est.)</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-est" href="#rank-9" style="--sw:4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Gong</span> <span class="soc-en">(Artisans)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 3–6% (est.)</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-est" href="#rank-10" style="--sw:4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Shang</span> <span class="soc-en">(Merchants)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 2–5% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:89%;--k:6"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-outcast soc-est" href="#rank-11" style="--sw:2%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Jianmin</span> <span class="soc-en">(The mean people)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 1–3% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:100%;--k:7"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-enslaved soc-est" href="#rank-12" style="--sw:1%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Nubi</span> <span class="soc-en">(Enslaved people)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 0.5–2% (est.)</span></a></li></ol><p class="soc-scale-note">By rank, the ladder is drawn as a pyramid. To scale, each bar is as wide as that rank&#8217;s share of the people. Shares marked est. are estimates where no count survives; their boxes and bars are dashed.</p></section>
<button type="button" class="kd-story soc-kd-open">Read the whole story</button>
<section class="soc-ranks" aria-label="The ranks, from the top"><article class="soc-rank soc-b-ruler" id="rank-1"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/wanli-emperor" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/wanli-emperor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Ruler</span> · <span>One ruler</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Huangdi</span> <span class="soc-en">(The emperor)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">The emperor ruled all of China from the Forbidden City in Beijing, with ministers and palace servants to help him.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The emperor stood on a social and legal tier above everyone else, embodying a heavenly mandate to judicial and executive authority. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandate-of-heaven">Mandate of Heaven</a> the right to rule rested on virtue, not birth: the Ming founder, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Hongwu Emperor</a>, had been a penniless peasant and Buddhist monk. In 1380 he executed his chancellor, abolished the office and took charge of the Six Ministries himself. Later emperors worked through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-secretariat">Grand Secretariat</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanli-emperor">Wanli Emperor</a> (r. 1572–1620) began with able advisers, among them the Grand Secretary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-juzheng">Zhang Juzheng</a>, but grew tired of his quarrelling ministers and stayed behind the walls of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbidden-city">Forbidden City</a>, where officials had to bribe powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> to have their messages carried to him.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> The throne passed within the founder’s house, and the reigning emperor named his heir: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Hongwu Emperor</a> chose his grandson. It could also be won by war, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a> took it from his nephew in 1402, and lost by capture: when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirats</a> seized the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhengtong-emperor">Zhengtong Emperor</a> in 1449 his brother took the throne, and he won it back only by a coup in 1457.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> One man, with the imperial family.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Hongwu Emperor</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanli-emperor">Wanli Emperor</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiajing-emperor">Jiajing Emperor</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandate-of-heaven">Mandate of Heaven</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-secretariat">Grand Secretariat</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbidden-city">Forbidden City</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-2"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/ming-china-2.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>Fewer than 1 in 1,000 (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Wang</span> <span class="soc-en">(Imperial princes)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">The emperor’s relatives were made princes with big estates and pay, but they were not allowed to govern.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Descendants of the first Ming emperor were made princes and given military commands, usually nominal, annual stipends and large estates. Their title was “king” (wang), but unlike the princes of the Han and Jin dynasties they held no feudatories: they served no administrative function and took part in military affairs only under the first two emperors. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a>, himself once the Prince of Yan, disarmed his brothers and moved their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> away from the militarized northern border. The princes, the consorts of imperial princesses and ennobled relatives staffed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-clan-court">Imperial Clan Court</a>, which kept the imperial genealogy. Some turned to learning: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-zaiyu">Zhu Zaiyu</a>, a sixth-generation descendant of the Hongxi Emperor, discovered equal temperament in music.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Princely rank came by descent from the founder, and the emperor could take a prince’s power away, as Yongle did his brothers’. Only near the end of the dynasty were some princes allowed to sit the examinations and serve as officials.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: the descendants of the founder made princes, with the consorts of imperial princesses and the ennobled relatives; the articles give no count.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-zaiyu">Zhu Zaiyu</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-clan-court">Imperial Clan Court</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: The robe of the Ming prince Zhu Tan, from his tomb (Shandong Provincial Museum) · Gary Todd from Xinzheng, China · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prince's_Robe%2C_Tomb_of_Ming_Prince_Zhu_Tan_(10144889466).jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-3"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/scholar-officials" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/scholar-officials.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>c. 0.05–0.1% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Shidafu</span> <span class="soc-en">(Officials)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Officials who had passed very hard exams on old books ran the government, from ministers to county magistrates.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">After 1384, when the founder stopped staffing his bureaus by recommendation only, the officials were recruited through the examinations. They were classed in nine grades, each of two degrees, with salaries nominally paid in piculs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>. The Six Ministries, of Personnel, Revenue, Rites, War, Justice and Public Works, did the government’s work, and the Grand Secretaries, drawn from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanlin-academy">Hanlin Academy</a>, coordinated them. Every three years senior officials graded their juniors, who were promoted, kept or demoted; the longest tenure in one office was nine years, and censors could impeach an official at any time. A district magistrate could not serve in his home district.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Office came through the examinations: provincial graduates began in low posts, while those who passed the palace examination became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinshi">jinshi</a> and were assured high positions. There were only two to four thousand jinshi at any time, “on the order of one out of 10,000 adult males”, in Ebrey’s words.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: Charles Hucker counted perhaps 100,000 lesser functionaries, who outnumbered the officials four to one, so roughly 25,000 officials; with their families perhaps 0.05–0.1 per cent of 160–200 million.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-yangming">Wang Yangming</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-juzheng">Zhang Juzheng</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xu-guangqi">Xu Guangqi</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scholar-officials">Scholar-officials</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-examination">Imperial examination</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinshi">Jinshi</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-secretariat">Grand Secretariat</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/censorate">Censorate</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanlin-academy">Hanlin Academy</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-specialists" id="rank-4"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/ming-china-4.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Soldiers, merchants and craftsmen</span> · <span>Fewer than 1 in 1,000</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Huanguan</span> <span class="soc-en">(Palace eunuchs)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">taijian</p><p class="soc-kid">Palace servants called eunuchs ran the emperor’s household, and some became more powerful than his ministers.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">Eunuchs</a> gained unprecedented power under the Ming. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Hongwu Emperor</a> forbade them to read or to meddle in politics, but from Yongle’s reign they ran huge imperial workshops, commanded armies, went as envoys and took part in appointing and promoting officials; the eunuch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He</a> led seven great voyages into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>. They had a bureaucracy of their own, parallel to the civil service, with ranks in four grades, and the Directorate of Ceremonial oversaw the secret police of the Eastern and Western Depots. In the 1590s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanli-emperor">Wanli Emperor</a> let them collect provincial taxes. Officials depicted them as greedy and cunning, and castration broke the Confucian duty to leave a male heir.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Many men castrated themselves for a place in the palace or in an official’s household, for them the only way into a life of privilege; others were prisoners of war, tribute from neighbouring lands, or boys punished for their fathers’ crimes. A few rose to rule the court, as Wang Zhen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-jin">Liu Jin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-zhongxian">Wei Zhongxian</a> did.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> About 70,000 eunuchs served the emperor at the end of the dynasty, and 100,000 at their height, among 160–200 million people.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-zhongxian">Wei Zhongxian</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-jin">Liu Jin</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">Eunuch</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: Palace eunuchs of the Xuande Emperor (r. 1425–1435) · Unknown court artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ming_Emperor_Xuande_Eunuchs_(1).jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-5"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/jinshi" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jinshi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>c. 0.2–0.5% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Shenshi</span> <span class="soc-en">(The <span class="soc-nw">degree-holding</span> gentry)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">shengyuan (&quot;government students&quot;), juren, jinshi</p><p class="soc-kid">Men who passed the first exams became gentry, the leaders of their towns and villages.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Below the officials stood the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a>, landholding families whose men had earned examination degrees: by the 16th century some 100,000 shengyuan, “government students”, the lowest tier of graduates, with the provincial and metropolitan graduates above them. The examinations tested the Confucian classics, above all the Four Books outlined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a>, and from 1487 the intricate “eight-legged essay”. Since only a select few could become officials, most of the gentry stayed at home as local leaders: they ran charities and private schools, helped settle minor disputes, supervised community projects, kept order, led Confucian ceremonies and helped collect taxes, and the magistrate depended on their advice. Some kept company with well-educated courtesans outside marriage.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> In theory anyone could sit the examinations, which were graded without regard to background, but the years of costly tutoring confined success to families of means: 90 per cent of the population was ineligible for lack of education, while the upper 10 per cent had equal chances. Provincial quotas curbed the hold of the landholding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> of the most prosperous regions.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: about 100,000 shengyuan, the lowest tier of graduates, by the 16th century, with the higher degree-holders and their families.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">Gentry</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-examination">Imperial examination</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinshi">Jinshi</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eight-legged-essay">Eight-legged essay</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juren">Juren</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-specialists" id="rank-6"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/weisuo-system" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/weisuo-system.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Soldiers, merchants and craftsmen</span> · <span>c. 1–3% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Soldiers and military households</span></h3><p class="soc-term">weisuo (guards and battalions), wu (the martial)</p><p class="soc-kid">Soldier families farmed land near their forts and served in the army, generation after generation.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Hongwu Emperor</a> built a standing army of more than a million men on the weisuo system: hereditary soldier households in garrisons (wei) and battalions (suo), settled on their own farm lots so that the army would feed itself. Military officers were graded like officials and evaluated every five years, but held less prestige, both because their service was hereditary and because Confucian values set the cultured pursuits (wen) above the profession of violence (wu). In the early Ming men of noble lineage held the higher commands; later, men of humbler origin displaced them. The hereditary army decayed through desertion, and by 1644 the soldiers were unpaid and unfed.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Soldier households were bound to service by birth, and many deserted; yet for a man without formal education the army was the quickest road to power, and officers could sit the civil examinations, or from 1478 the military’s own.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: the founder’s standing army exceeded one million troops; with their hereditary households a few million people, though desertion had thinned the ranks long before the 16th century.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-sangui">Wu Sangui</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weisuo-system">Weisuo system</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-religious" id="rank-7"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/taoism" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/taoism.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Religious and learned</span> · <span>c. 0.2–0.5% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Buddhist and Daoist clergy</span></h3><p class="soc-term">-</p><p class="soc-kid">Buddhist monks and Daoist priests looked after temples, and the government kept lists of them.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Monks and priests stood outside the four occupations; a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> official had counted Buddhist monks as a sixth category beside them. The Ministry of Rites kept the registers of the Buddhist and Daoist priesthoods. The early Ming emperors favoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Taoism</a> and gave its practitioners many posts in the state’s ritual offices, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiajing-emperor">Jiajing Emperor</a> (r. 1521–1567) persecuted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> at court in favour of Taoism. Temples, like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a>, were exempt from tax, and farmers “donated” their lands to them to escape it. In the late Ming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> arrived, the most influential of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matteo-ricci">Matteo Ricci</a>, who worked with the convert <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xu-guangqi">Xu Guangqi</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> incident of 1616 led to the exile of four Jesuits to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a>.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> The monastic life’s exemption from tax had long drawn poor farmers to it; the state answered with registers and, at times, persecution.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: the Buddhist monks and Daoist priests on the Ministry of Rites’ registers, with the few Jesuit missionaries of the late Ming; the articles give no count.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Taoism</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-8"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/huai-river" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/huai-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>About 86% (the rest, est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Nong</span> <span class="soc-en">(Farmers)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">liangmin (good people)</p><p class="soc-kid">Most people were farmers who grew rice, wheat, tea or silk and paid taxes to the emperor.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Farmers ranked second in theory, after the scholars, as the primary creators of wealth, and the land tax on their plots produced much of the state’s revenue. North of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huai-river">Huai River</a> most were owner-cultivators who grew wheat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> and lived not far above subsistence; to the south were wealthy landlords and tenant farmers, intensive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> fields, and mulberry trees for silkworms and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> bushes south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">Maize</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potatoes</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> diminished famines. Farmers paid their taxes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> but sold their crops for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, and as more families disappeared from the registers or “donated” their lands to tax-exempt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> and temples, the revenue was squeezed out of fewer and fewer farmers.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> A prospering family could pay for a son’s schooling, though the cost kept most out of the examinations. In hard times farmers went to the cities for work, and many left their registered homes to wander, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiajing-emperor">Jiajing Emperor</a> had officials register migrants wherever they had moved.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Everyone not counted in the other ranks: chiefly farming families, the great majority. Several of the other ranks are estimates, so this share is one too.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huai-river">Huai River</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-9"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/ming-china-9.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>c. 3–6% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Gong</span> <span class="soc-en">(Artisans)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Craftspeople made silk, paper and blue-and-white porcelain, some of it sold as far away as Europe.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Artisans came third in the four occupations, respected more than merchants in theory because they made useful things, though, often having no land of their own, they paid little to the state. Their skills were handed down orally from father to son. Some worked for the government: the Ministry of Public Works hired artisans and labourers for temporary service, and the imperial kilns at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jingdezhen">Jingdezhen</a> made the famous blue and white <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>, while the Dehua factories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> made export porcelain for European tastes by the late 16th century. In late Ming workshops in textiles, paper and other industries, small teams worked for wages under a master craftsman. Artisans formed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> of their own.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> A skilled artisan could gain enough capital to hire apprentices and labourers and run a small enterprise; occupations could be chosen or inherited, for the four occupations were not hereditary.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: the craftsmen of the towns, the workshops and the imperial kilns; the articles give no count.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">Porcelain</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jingdezhen">Jingdezhen</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: A blue-and-white porcelain plate of the Yongle reign · Windmemories · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20241025_Blue_and_White_Porcelain_Plate_of_Yongle_Reign%2C_Ming_Dynasty.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-10"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/ming-china-10.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>c. 2–5% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Shang</span> <span class="soc-en">(Merchants)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Merchants were ranked last in old ideas, but many grew rich trading salt, silk and tea.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Merchants stood last in theory, distrusted for piling up wealth, and the early Ming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumptuary-law">sumptuary</a> laws barred them and their families from wearing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>; it was frowned upon for merchants to sit the examinations. Yet the wealthy merchants and the landholding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> were fusing, a trend that culminated in the late Ming. Officials needed merchants’ money for roads, schools, bridges, pagodas and book-making; the court granted merchants licences to trade in salt in return for grain shipped to the northern garrisons; and merchants pooled their capital in associations (huiguan, gongsuo) and partnerships and invested their profits in land. They bought guides to proper conduct and business ethics, and many officials now named merchant relatives in their family histories.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Wealth carried a merchant’s family toward the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a>: merchants funded schools and imitated the scholars, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumptuary-law">sumptuary</a> laws were no longer enforced from the mid-Ming, and in some regions scholars switched to careers in trade.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: from salt merchants and merchant bankers to shopkeepers and travelling peddlers; the articles give no count.</p></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: Market stalls at the Lantern Festival, from a Ming court painting of 1485 · Imperial Ming Court · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ming_Emperor_Xianzong_Enjoying_the_Lantern_Festival_(Ming_Dynasty)_(mamianqun_and_yesa).jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-outcast" id="rank-11"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/ming-china-11.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Outcast groups</span> · <span>c. 1–3% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Jianmin</span> <span class="soc-en">(The mean people)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Entertainers such as musicians and dancers were looked down on by law, even when audiences loved them.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Beneath the four occupations were the “mean people” (jianmin), set against the “good people” (liangmin): those whose work was thought worthless or “filthy”, entertainers and prostitutes above all. They were not registered as commoners and bore legal disabilities. Musicians who played as their full-time work were of low status, and many dancers had entered the trade through poverty. Yet female musicians were so common under the Ming that they played even for imperial rituals, wealthy families kept private <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> troupes, and educated courtesans (geji) were as skilled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a>, painting and poetry as their male guests. Virtually every town had a brothel where female and male prostitutes could be had.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> A few dancers rose by becoming concubines, but the law kept the mean people apart: to give entertainers official recognition would have given them more prestige.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: entertainers, musicians, prostitutes and others in work thought worthless or “filthy”; the articles give no count.</p></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: Guo Xu, Xie An with Courtesans at Dongshan, 1526 · Guo Xu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E4%B8%9C%E5%B1%B1%E6%90%BA%E5%A6%93%E5%9B%BE.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-enslaved" id="rank-12"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/ming-china-12.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Enslaved</span> · <span>c. 0.5–2% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Nubi</span> <span class="soc-en">(Enslaved people)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Some people were enslaved, often as a punishment for crimes, even though the first Ming emperor tried to end slavery.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> was comparatively uncommon in Chinese history and was practised largely as a judicial punishment for crimes. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Hongwu Emperor</a> tried to ban it entirely, without success. Children of poor families were often enslaved illegally under the guise of adoption, and the state made slaves of rebels: when Miao tribes revolted, a Ming governor castrated thousands of their boys and gave them as slaves to officials, for which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhengtong-emperor">Tianshun Emperor</a> reprimanded and condemned him. Castration also punished a family’s crimes: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> Huai’en (d. 1488) was castrated as a boy because his father and kinsmen, government officials, were accused of crimes.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> People fell into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> by a criminal sentence, by the sale of poor children disguised as adoption, or as captives of war and rebellion.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: slavery was comparatively uncommon and largely a judicial punishment; to it were added children sold under the guise of adoption and captives given to officials; the articles give no count.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: Shen Shichong, Man and Servant Beneath Trees, 1616 (Berkeley Art Museum) · Shen Shichong · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shen_Shichong.Man_and_Servant_Beneath_Trees._1616._Collection_the_University_of_California%2C_Berkeley_Art_Museum.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article></section>
<section class="soc-born" id="born" aria-labelledby="soc-born-t"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-born-t">Born into history</h2><p class="soc-born-lede">Where would you have stood? Chance places you on the ladder, as birth placed most people: weighted by how many each rank held.</p><button type="button" class="soc-born-btn" data-born="ming-china">Be born in Ming China</button><div class="soc-born-out" aria-live="polite"></div></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Moving up and down</h2><p>The examinations were the great road up, and historians debate whether they widened or narrowed it. They were graded without regard to background and were open in theory to everyone, but success took years of costly tutoring, so 90 per cent of the people were shut out for lack of education, while the upper tenth had equal chances; provincial quotas spread the degrees beyond the richest regions, and cheap printed classics and model answers widened the pool of candidates. For a man without education the army was the quickest way up, and for some the palace: men castrated themselves to serve as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a>. Wealth also moved families, as merchants funded schools, bought books on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> manners and merged with the landholding gentry. Ideas shook the order too: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-yangming">Wang Yangming</a> taught that anyone, whatever his pedigree, could become as wise as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>, his disciple Wang Gen, a salt-mine worker, lectured commoners on education, and people began to question whether the scholar should stand above the farmer. Falls were common as well: farmers who fled the tax registers became drifters and migrant workers, and in the 1640s starving peasants who could not pay their taxes formed huge bands of rebels, and in 1644 the rebel army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-zicheng">Li Zicheng</a>, a peasant soldier, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>.</p></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Women on the ladder</h2><p>Women could not sit the examinations, so their standing followed their fathers’ and husbands’. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">censuses</a> undercounted women and girls: in 1502 the Daming Prefecture reported 378,167 males and 226,982 females, and contemporary authors described female infanticide as “rampant”. The early Ming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumptuary-law">sumptuary</a> laws forbade women ornaments of gold, jade, pearl or emerald. In the palace, women first staffed their own bureaus of attendance, ceremonies, apparel, food and handicrafts, but from the 1420s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> took over all but the Bureau of Apparel. Women of educated families read the new vernacular novels, and some mothers and courtesans were as literate and skilled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a>, painting and poetry as men; the thinker Li Zhi taught that women were men’s intellectual equals and should be better educated, and died in prison for “dangerous ideas”. In the entertainment trades women were classed among the mean people, and the daughters of poor families could be sold under the guise of adoption.</p></section>
