<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Sources and further reading</h2><ul class="essay-refs"><li>Elman, B. A. (2016) &quot;Civil Service Examinations Kējǔ 科举&quot;, in Cheng, L. (ed.) Berkshire Encyclopedia of China, pp. 405–410.</li><li>Farmer, E. L. (ed.) (1994) Ming History: An Introductory Guide to Research.</li><li>Li, S. (2018) &quot;Scholar-Officials&quot;, in Li, S. and Ryden, E. (eds.) The Constitution of Ancient China. Princeton University Press, pp. 98–138.</li><li>Zhang, D. (2020) &quot;Scholar-Officials: Struggling for the Right Position&quot;, in Thriving in Crisis: Buddhism and Political Disruption in China, 1522–1620. Columbia University Press, pp. 119–155.</li><li>Zhang, Z. (1955) The Chinese Gentry; Studies on Their Role in Nineteenth-Century Chinese Society. Seattle: University of Washington Press.</li></ul><p class="soc-method">The shares are of the whole population, children included. Where a scholar has counted or estimated a rank, its share follows that work, named on the rank; where no count survives, the share is an estimate, marked est. and drawn dashed, made from what the rank was and did, from related figures and from the same rank in comparable societies, and its note says what it rests on. Population: late-Ming estimates of Fairbank (160 million), Brook (175 million) and Ebrey (200 million); the official censuses after 1393 counted only 51–62 million.</p></section>
